---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE_AUTHOR, SITE_NAME } from '../config/site';
import { METHODOLOGY_PATH } from '../config/editorial';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${SITE_NAME} — practical home project guides, material reference articles, and transparent planning calculators for DIYers.`}
  path="/about"
>
  <p slot="lede" class="section-lede">
    What {SITE_NAME} publishes, who it is for, and how the guides and calculators fit together.
  </p>

  <h2>What is FixUpWise?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>{SITE_NAME}</strong> is a home-improvement publication focused on project planning math and
    practical material guidance. It helps homeowners and DIYers estimate quantities, understand common
    coverage and yield figures, and prepare shopping lists before they buy.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is it for?</h2>
  <p>
    Homeowners and DIYers who need clear planning numbers — slab volume, paint gallons, mulch depth,
    tile counts, and similar quantity questions — without treating a webpage as engineering advice or a
    contractor bid.
  </p>

  <h2>What does FixUpWise publish?</h2>
  <p>
    Editorial guides and reference articles across projects, materials, and how-to topics, plus
    interactive material calculators. Content is attributed to <strong>{SITE_AUTHOR}</strong>. Articles
    use editorial formats such as worked examples, decision guides, and product references so the job of
    each page is clear.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the calculators for?</h2>
  <p>
    The calculators estimate quantities for concrete, gravel, mulch, paint, and tile. They apply labeled
    formulas and defaults you can change — coverage rates, waste factors, bag sizes, and similar inputs —
    so you can compare scenarios before you shop.
  </p>

  <h2>How are calculations different from project recommendations?</h2>
  <p>
    A calculation answers “how much material does this size and depth imply?” A recommendation answers
    “how thick should your slab be?” or “how deep should you mulch?” {SITE_NAME} keeps those apart:
    quantity math is deterministic for the inputs you enter; thickness, depth, and product choices remain
    planning context with labeled assumptions — not code approval or professional design.
  </p>

  <h2>How is content researched?</h2>
  <p>
    Topics come from common homeowner planning questions. Calculator formulas and yield/coverage figures
    are checked against manufacturer documentation and authoritative public sources such as university
    extension guidance where those sources apply. Assumptions are labeled on the page. {SITE_NAME} does
    not invent field tests, expert credentials, or contractor review.
  </p>
  <p>
    Full process, sourcing rules, illustration labeling, and update practice:
    <a href={METHODOLOGY_PATH}>How We Create Content</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related: <a href="/contact">Contact</a> · <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a>
  </p>
</PageLayout>
